WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.240
Without a doubt, Oberyn Martell is the best
one-off character in Game of Thrones.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.740
And Bobby B exists so there’s tough competition
for that title.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.250
Oberyn I think is the most universally likable
character in the entire show.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:18.120
This is because of his great personality with
unbreakable charisma, along with a deep well

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.930
of other lovable personality traits.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.119
Then he’s brought to life perfectly by Pedro
Pascal and this was really his breakthrough

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:27.310
performance as an actor.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.530
Before this, he had minor roles in television,
so he proved himself here and it opened a

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.690
gateway into getting better roles.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.390
It just seems like he plays Oberyn effortlessly
and with so much confidence to the point that

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.600
you couldn’t see anyone else as that character.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.030
The first area I want to tackle for understanding
why Oberyn is such a perfect character is

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.030
the writing.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.280
And then we can go into more detail about
Pedro’s performance and then the external

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.710
factors about the character that make him
even better.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.370
Upon rewatching all of Oberyn’s scenes,
I realized each one of them introduces and

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.920
showcases a new personality trait.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:06.400
It makes his scenes feel very efficient because
we keep learning new aspects tied to the character.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.890
Going through his scenes, we have his opening
where it’s shown he’s a courageous, fearless,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.890
badass.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.800
His scene with Tyrion shows him to be vengeful.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.540
He’s able to suavely play politics at Joffrey’s
wedding, He’s a compassionate lover who

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.150
wants to experience all of the desires in
the world, he has strong composure and doesn’t

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:28.170
act on impulse in the face of Tywin, he’s
a kind intellectual who writes poems and treats

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.619
Myrcella with care, the small council meeting
shows him to have an air of disobedience,

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:38.390
and with all of this, he’s even able to
be sincere with Tyrion in the dungeons.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.370
Almost all of his scenes are productive on
a character development level.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.440
And even though there’s a proficiency in
character development, the writers don’t

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.299
compromise any plot development and are able
to expertly do both.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.259
Like an example being his first scene in which
it gives you the entire essence of Oberyn

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:57.820
while also explaining his entire motivation
and backstory.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:05.829
So right away you understand the character.

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.530
I feel like this isn’t something to take
for granted because there are so many other

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:14.530
shows in which characters have way more screen
time than Oberyn, but yet, they are significantly

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:15.740
more dull.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.300
Oberyn’s scenes are meticulously planned
and aspects of his character are always being

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.690
revealed to the viewer.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.330
It makes all of his scenes much more entertaining
and thus makes him even more alluring.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:30.200
Through all of his screen time, you are also
searching for any character flaws and negative

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.630
traits and that finally shows up in his last
scene.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.860
During his fight with the Mountain, he gets
overconfident and thus becomes careless in

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.640
the pursuit of the truth.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.250
So even though he’s a very colorful character
with many likable traits, he’s still a flawed

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.470
individual and that makes him human.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:51.410
By his final scene, it comes full circle and
this final trait finishes his character and

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.080
his overall arc.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.470
Just a very efficient set-up and pay-off in
the course of only eight episodes.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.410
That’s an achievement in of itself from
how great of a character George was able to

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.390
write in that short amount of time.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:08.140
Another clever thing I picked up on on rewatch
was how much the writers emphasized the mystery

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.140
of Oberyn.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.940
All of his opening scenes generally paint
him as more of a villain because he openly

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.080
says that he hates the Lannisters.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.340
At the ending of each of these scenes, they
generally end with an open threat.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.970
Like the way, his first interaction ends with
Tyrion.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.080
This is also the first episode of the season,
so at this point in the story, you assume

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.480
that Oberyn will be a possible catalyst to
blow up conflict in Kingslanding.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.900
Throughout the show, we are taught not to
trust anyone.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.300
So this new character showing up according
to things we learned earlier and general story

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.270
tropes, tells us that Oberyn is not to be
trusted.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.739
Especially since he openly made his objective
well known.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:47.920
So the twist in which he actually turns out
to be a good guy is that much more surprising

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.070
because he shattered our expectations of this
new mysterious character.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.750
That and this whole scene was brilliantly
written.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:59.019
Easily one of my favorite monologue scenes
that is imbued with so much emotion.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:03.280
Up until this point, we’ve never seen Oberyn
in this more sensitive light, so seeing him

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.550
have a moving scene with Tyrion was special.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:09.799
Along with it even adding in more backstory
about how Cersei and other people hyped up

00:04:09.799 --> 00:04:14.480
Tyrion as being a hideous monster as a baby
when in reality he wasn’t.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.919
It makes for a great point for Oberyn to sympathize
with Tyrion and him choosing to be his champion

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:21.970
was one of the most uplifting moments in Game
of Thrones.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:27.610
Tyrion at this point in the story has utterly
lost and even his own brother and best friend

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.880
won’t fight for him, so Oberyn coming in
out of left field to help him was a great

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:32.880
twist.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.070
Which if you’ve been paying attention, this
perfectly makes sense within the story because

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.919
of Oberyn’s vendetta against the Mountain.

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:43.669
But yet, it doesn’t come across as an obvious
point in the story because the Mountain is

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:47.340
so menacing and terrifying to the point of
no one wanting to fight him.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.289
So overall, this is really the turning point
for Oberyn’s character that really makes

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:53.340
the audience rally for him.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.650
This is his purpose within the show and now
he’s extremely important.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.470
The amount of stakes tied to his character
has drastically surged and the upcoming mountain

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.850
fight makes you hyped as hell.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:07.620
From here we can look at the Mountain vs.
the Viper scene and obviously, it has some

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.130
fun action, but what really floored me was
the writing.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:16.160
The twist at the end where Oberyn gets overconfident
and gets blindsided by his vengeance for Tywin,

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.540
and makes a misstep that costs him his life
is probably the most shocking twist right

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.160
behind the Red Wedding.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.669
The reason why this twist rocks you so hard
is because it was very unpredictable and because

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:28.240
of Tyrion’s fate.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.840
For the last 3 seasons, Tyrion has been the
primary protagonist in Kingslanding.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.039
So going into this trial by combat, you assume
that there is a fair chance that Tyrion will

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:41.970
survive given his importance, along with Oberyn
being this new and dominant character.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.530
So surely they won’t kill him right?

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:45.530
Right!??

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:49.080
Then to even combat the suspicion that Oberyn
will lose, they actually make it to where

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.759
he wins, and then in a freak accident, he
dies.

00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:56.830
This is literally the best outcome from a
writing perspective and I can’t imagine

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:58.729
a better way to have written this scene.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:02.710
It’s a roller coaster in which you experience
the highs of winning and the lows of losing.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:07.020
It’s a massive rug pull just when you think
all the danger is gone.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.180
It feels like George RR.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:12.470
Martin is playing mind games with you because
you know the most logical twist will be for

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:17.050
Oberyn to win, but then that in turn now seems
like a predictable outcome.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:21.900
George somehow accounts for this and blows
us away with what ends up happening.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.240
Along with him obliterating the status quo
of the show because as an audience, we assume

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.370
that Tyrion will return to what he’s been
doing in Kingslanding.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:33.910
So by completely sending Tyrion in an unknown
direction, it leaves viewers not being able

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:39.069
to predict where the story goes, and thus,
this was the least expected outcome.

00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:43.240
Another crazy thing is that Oberyn not killing
the Mountain is not contrived.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.720
Oberyn is trying to desperately question the
Mountain for information and this makes sense

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.280
because this objective has been established
in multiple scenes.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.860
In order to subvert expectations in a narrative
it needs to make sense within the story because

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.180
here it works expertly.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.289
Meanwhile, in the later seasons, it utterly
shatters the logic in order to pull off hollow

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:03.879
shocking moments.

00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:07.870
Like Rhaegal’s death scene being the biggest
offender where Dany forgets about the iron

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.840
fleet despite being reminded of it and getting
sniped out of the sky.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.229
This may quote on quote shock the viewer,
but it’s met with confusion and anger because

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:19.889
it compromises any realistic notion within
the story.

00:07:19.889 --> 00:07:21.569
In essence, it’s bullshit.

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:25.300
And David and Dan keep doing this throughout
seasons 7 and 8.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:30.210
So Oberyn’s final scene is the literal opposite
of this because it’s logical and is driven

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.669
by two amazing characters.

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:36.099
I know I took some of the emphasis off of
Oberyn and talked about this overall scene,

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:38.970
but it clearly wouldn’t be possible without
Oberyn.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:43.650
The reason why his death hits so hard is because
he was such a lovable character who was taken

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.129
away so soon.

00:07:45.129 --> 00:07:49.560
His presence was short and sweet, and because
of this, he’s a straight-up perfect character

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.349
because his time in the story has absolutely
no downtime or flaws.

00:07:54.349 --> 00:07:58.640
Like it’s a lot harder to call other characters
perfect considering their trajectory for the

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.460
later seasons, unless if you just ignore their
later seasons altogether.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:07.440
Another thing about Oberyn’s scenes is that
he contrasts really well to certain characters,

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.210
and the writers take advantage of that.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:13.919
Like one of my favorite pairings was Oberyn
talking to Varys in the throne room.

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:18.509
Oberyn is a man who loves pursuing desires
and is constantly indulging himself at every

00:08:18.509 --> 00:08:20.340
opportunity he can get.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:25.620
So this being contrasted to someone who has
no sexual desires genuinely confounds Oberyn.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:30.030
And Varys even says that he’s had no desires
even before being a eunuch.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.620
This is the antithesis as to how Oberyn operates
and their interaction because of this was

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.140
very engaging.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:40.110
The part in the script where Oberyn asks Varys
what his true desire is given his lack of

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.820
sexual ambition, and all he does is look over
at the Iron Throne and is a genius point in

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:45.970
the script.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:49.700
This kind of contrast can also be seen in
his small council scene.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.790
What is a man who is so disobedient doing
in a setting in which you have to serve the

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:54.790
hand.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:59.360
All the characters here want to kiss Tywin’s
ass, while Oberyn definitely does not.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.209
The rivalry between Oberyn and Mace Tyrell
is really funny and it shows the stark differences

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:07.070
between what these characters value so much.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.260
One is a boot licker who will do anything
for Tywin, while the other refuses to stand

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:14.210
when he enters the room and is lounging in
his seat.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:18.779
These kind of scenarios the writers put Oberyn
into are so intriguing because he sticks out

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:21.420
much more from these major differences.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:26.490
It amplifies his characteristics and ideologies
because they aren’t normal in this setting.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.060
So they essentially stand out like a sore
thumb.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:33.920
On the ideology front, his quick snappy exchange
with Cersei about how Dorne doesn’t despise

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.390
bastards creates a good conflicting point
for these characters to bud heads on.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:43.200
So by putting Oberyn into settings that fundamentally
contrast his character, it puts a spotlight

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.630
onto what makes him so original.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:49.650
From a writing point of view, I think Oberyn
is the best example you can pull on, as to

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:54.000
how to expertly set up and literally execute
a new character.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.900
From every scene being very productive in
what they teach us about Oberyn and his personality,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.530
playing into the mysterious angle of his character
to make him more intriguing, contrasting him

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:08.420
to certain characters and settings to amplify
his characteristics, and every twist he’s

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.360
involved in perfectly utilizes him as a character.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.399
Truly a flawless character who gets even better
from an acting standpoint from what Pedro

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:18.320
Pascal brings to the table.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:19.320
You know what?

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:20.610
Let’s do a little test.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:25.529
Do you think Pedro Pascal won any awards for
playing Oberyn Martell or at the very least

00:10:25.529 --> 00:10:26.529
was nominated?

00:10:26.529 --> 00:10:31.890
The answer is surprisingly no and not even
any nominations.

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:36.459
But between most of the characters in this
season, I think Pedro Pascal’s screen presence

00:10:36.459 --> 00:10:38.130
was so infectious.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:42.589
He nailed every characteristic of Oberyn perfectly
and there was a lot to nail.

00:10:42.589 --> 00:10:46.529
As I said before, every scene essentially
showcases a new characteristic.

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:50.800
So from an acting standpoint, it’s really
impressive to me that Pedro Pascal was able

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.490
to gracefully portray each of these characteristics
so well.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:59.279
This also requires a ton of range from Pedro
because he has to show heaps of charisma,

00:10:59.279 --> 00:11:04.040
confidence, stoicness, and on the opposite
side of the spectrum he has to be vulnerable.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:08.209
If it wasn’t for his monologue with Tyrion,
he would have just been a fun character, but

00:11:08.209 --> 00:11:11.260
this scene really cements him as an emotional
powerhouse.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.339
This kind of reminds me of Robert Baratheon.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:18.310
In most of his scenes, he’s either being
a strict ruler or a memeing menace, but the

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:22.630
scene where he gets real with Cersei and talks
about their marriage in detail really unveils

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.779
a hidden side to his character.

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:28.779
This is what Oberyn’s scene with Tyrion
is to me, and is even better because it has

00:11:28.779 --> 00:11:31.529
a strong progression throughout the entire
scene.

00:11:31.529 --> 00:11:36.100
Pedro is able to build to the point of sincere
sadness by first starting out in a more playful

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.100
mood.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.579
He starts out cracking some jokes and we first
think he’s chastising Tyrion when he talks

00:11:40.579 --> 00:11:52.770
about how people called him a devilish baby.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:57.310
Then slowly through this captivating monologue,
he sympathizes and feels bad for Tyrion to

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.070
the point of where he’s shedding a tear.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:04.139
It’s an extremely cathartic moment and this
low-key scene again shows Oberyn’s more

00:12:04.139 --> 00:12:05.529
heartwarming side.

00:12:05.529 --> 00:12:11.390
So overall for his range, it’s showcased
in spades and he’s more than a badass warrior.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:15.440
For another aspect about his acting that I
feel like is hard to achieve, is the amount

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.399
of charisma he portrays.

00:12:17.399 --> 00:12:21.920
This is an aspect that a lot of leading actors
can lack and it becomes very noticeable if

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.720
the actor doesn’t feel confident in the
character.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.420
If they lack this confidence then their performance
will in turn become dull.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:33.060
But what makes an actor great, is if they
make you believe they truly are that character

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:36.100
and don’t come across as just reading lines.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:40.430
This requires a lot more involvement from
the actor and establishing more detailed aspects

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:45.180
like the voice of the character, inflection,
cadence, and the way they physically hold

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:46.180
themselves.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:50.630
You can make an easy distinction between great
and bad actors if each one of these areas

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.680
aren’t different for their characters.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:57.010
Like Daniel Day Lewis essentially re-writes
his entire genetic make-up when entering a

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:01.920
new character, and then on the other hand,
the Rock or Kevin Hart essentially play themselves

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:03.190
in every role.

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:07.500
Pedro Pascal as Oberyn feels very distinct
in each one of these areas.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.430
His voice has a unique accent given that he’s
from Dorne, his inflection is very particular,

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:17.300
and regularly puts emphasis on certain words
for bigger dramatic impact and he has the

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:22.100
cadence of a smooth river, and then physically
he simultaneously feels like an untouchable

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:26.410
god while also being very particular and smart
with how he moves.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:30.079
Like once you really start to think about
the work Pedro Pascal is putting in here,

00:13:30.079 --> 00:13:32.720
it’s like uncovering a deeper iceberg.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:36.760
From all of this meticulous care being put
into the character he comes across as very

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.320
comfortable and he thoroughly understands
him.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:43.899
So much to the point that he occasionally
adds in subtle improvisations that made his

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:45.420
character more real and natural.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:50.320
It’s kind of hard to identify improvisations
without access to the script, but I’ll show

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:51.769
a couple examples.

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:55.560
The first one is my favorite where Oberyn
gracefully swipes his hand through the wick

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.700
of a candle, which boosted his badass meter
by a huge degree.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:09.820
Then this one moment during Tywin’s dialogue
scene shows how lively Oberyn is.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:13.380
These spontaneous small actions breathe life
into the character.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:17.529
A lot of actors are just concerned about hitting
their marks for blocking and delivering lines

00:14:17.529 --> 00:14:20.070
to the point that they feel restricted in
a way.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:24.839
Pedro Pascal breathes instinctiveness into
his performance and through these improvisations,

00:14:24.839 --> 00:14:28.410
it communicates a visual language of the character
that he’s more natural.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:32.910
And he’s more natural because he’s actively
engaging and feeling confident in his environment,

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.450
which in turn aids his performance.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.649
Pedro also seems like he’s having a blast
playing the character in all of his scenes

00:14:39.649 --> 00:14:42.000
and that comes through very apparently.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.820
Going back to the opening of this segment,
it’s a shame that Pedro Pascal as Oberyn

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:50.610
didn’t get any award recognition which feels
ironic because of how iconic the character

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:51.610
is.

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:55.829
This literally put him on the map and I think
he deserved much more credit back then.

00:14:55.829 --> 00:14:59.930
To finish up the video, I quickly want to
go into one external factor that makes Oberyn

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:01.149
even better.

00:15:01.149 --> 00:15:04.570
At this point in Game of Thrones, we’ve
essentially hit the peak and in my opinion,

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.709
season 4 is the best season.

00:15:06.709 --> 00:15:11.400
Every character is completely fleshed out
and they are all amazing in their own way.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:16.009
So introducing a new character like Oberyn
I feel like would be a daunting task because

00:15:16.009 --> 00:15:20.890
you now have to make a character that is of
the same caliber of our pre-existing ones.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.820
And the main external reason that makes him
inherently so interesting is the fact that

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:25.870
he’s from Dorne.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:29.569
He offers a unique perspective in the already
vast world of Game of Thrones.

00:15:29.569 --> 00:15:34.250
We’ve essentially seen representation and
the different ways of life in most of Westeros

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.420
and out of all of them, Dorne is really unique.

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:41.940
The people there are untamed and are resilient
to normal customs and seeing Oberyn in the

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:46.250
setting of a place that doesn’t communicate
his values, creates some good conflict and

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:47.540
juxtaposition.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:52.170
We get to see some in-world racism like the
remarks the Lannister soldiers make towards

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:53.170
Oberyn.

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:57.170
This adds a lot of subtext about how people
view the dornish and this is very important

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.880
for how rich and huge the world is.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:03.850
Now Dorne in the long run doesn’t end up
being that important given that the season

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:09.170
5 subplot was terrible and their impact in
the later seasons was non-existent, but Oberyn

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:13.140
in season 4 sets a good introductory point
for the Dornish culture.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:17.410
So if the show happened to explore better
plotlines in Dorne, *clears throat* a better

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:21.890
book adaption of Dorne maybe, then this starting
point sets a good foundation to then explore

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:22.890
later on.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:28.300
It’s just too bad that it was kind of wasted
and even though it was, it does not diminish

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:30.389
Oberyn’s brilliance in season 4.

00:16:30.389 --> 00:16:34.780
Thank you for watching and I’m kind of curious
about how well this video performs.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:38.589
Because I still have more Game of Thrones
videos I can make and want to do, but if the

00:16:38.589 --> 00:16:43.180
interest around the show and viewership is
declining a lot, then I might prioritize making

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:44.620
other videos.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.870
There is a series I want to start at some
point this year that I think will be very

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.260
fun and there is a lot of potential there.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:52.449
But yeah, thank you again for watching, and
have a great day.

